<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja: An Offshore Wind Energy Hub in the Eastern Baltic Sea</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>As the geopolitical landscape, market conditions and people’s habits change rapidly, the need for carefully considered, innovative and environmentally friendly solutions is increasing. One current priority is the plan to build several offshore wind farms in the Baltic Sea from 2027. This is supported by international agreements, the Baltic Sea maritime spatial plan identifying suitable locations, and the need to strengthen energy independence.</p><p>A discussion entitled “Latvian Ports as the Foundation of Energy Independence” brought together representatives of Latvia’s major ports, the Latvian Wind Energy Association, Latvenergo management, the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, and major port stevedoring companies.</p><p>Transport Minister Jānis Vitenbergs said: “Offshore wind farms and energy independence are priorities for Latvia, and well-developed port infrastructure is essential. Today’s discussion showed which issues are urgent and require prompt, practical solutions to move wind farm projects and supporting infrastructure forward. We saw today that Liepāja has done significant preparatory work and developed an impressive proposal.”</p><p>Building offshore wind farms in the Baltic Sea requires a support base in a port. The Port of Liepāja is among the most suitable locations for this infrastructure. The city’s and Liepāja SEZ’s long-term development plans, including Strategic Objective No. 2 on developing the outer harbour, provide a foundation for taking action quickly and saving time before facilities are due to become operational in 2027.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ Manager Uldis Hmieļevskis said: “Currently, no port on the eastern coast of the Baltic Sea has the required infrastructure: at least 15–20 hectares of land with two 200-metre high-load-bearing berths. We believe that projects in Latvia should use Latvian port infrastructure. Liepāja has the available land within the harbour and favourable geotechnical conditions to provide the required load capacity. We have therefore developed an outer harbour concept with 39 hectares of infrastructure in the first phase. The technical solution, investment needs, implementation roadmap and schedule have been prepared. The project would take four years, fitting the region’s offshore wind farm construction plans and even allowing some additional time.”</p><p>The project aims to establish a major regional installation port for offshore wind farm construction, while also providing land for related manufacturing and an operations and maintenance (O&amp;M) base. The technical design draws on best practices from leading offshore wind service ports in Denmark, the Netherlands and Germany. Implementation is planned in three stages according to market demand, covering a total area of 88.5 hectares.</p><p>Wind energy manufacturers are already showing strong interest in opportunities offered by Liepāja SEZ. Plans for new Baltic Sea wind farms have stretched the capacity of existing factories, prompting a search for locations for new plants serving both offshore and onshore wind projects. Port infrastructure and logistics are vital to all these projects.</p></div>
